random: do not credit the bootloader's seed unless the command line says to

Faraday's own change, not upstream's. Since Linux 6.2 the kernel credits
the bootloader's seed by default, and `random.trust_bootloader=off` is the
only way to stop it. On 32-bit ARM the device tree's /chosen/rng-seed,
which the Pi's firmware always writes, is read and credited in
setup_machine_fdt, before parse_early_param has read the command line, so
the parameter comes too late and the pool is counted ready at the first
line of the log. Here the default is off: the seed is still mixed into the
pool, and only a command line with `random.trust_bootloader=on` credits it.

diff --git a/drivers/char/random.c b/drivers/char/random.c
--- a/drivers/char/random.c
+++ b/drivers/char/random.c
@@ -811,7 +811,7 @@
  **********************************************************************/
 
 static bool trust_cpu __initdata = true;
-static bool trust_bootloader __initdata = true;
+static bool trust_bootloader __initdata = false;
 static int __init parse_trust_cpu(char *arg)
 {
 	return kstrtobool(arg, &trust_cpu);
